# Build output
/secscan
/dist/
*.test

# Scanner output — NEVER commit this.
#
# A snapshot or state file is a complete map of a host's weaknesses: open
# findings, exposed ports, weak accounts, the command transcripts behind them.
# Committing one is a worse disclosure than any credential this repo could
# leak, and it is easy to do by accident by pointing SECSCAN_HOME at the
# working directory. ci/guards.sh fails the build if any of these appear.
/state.json
/history.jsonl
/audit.jsonl
/snapshots/
/data/
*.snapshot.json
*-assessment.md
secscan-report*.md

# Local configuration. Every setting is an environment variable (see
# .env.example, generated from the config registry), so a local .env is the
# natural place to keep a session secret or an Entra client secret.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.envrc

# Site-specific disclosure denylist: names of real hosts or internal projects
# that do not look like domains and so cannot be caught generically. Gitignored
# on purpose — a committed denylist publishes exactly the inventory it defends.
/ci/disclosure-denylist.local

# Keys and certificates, in case one is ever dropped here while testing
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*

.DS_Store
